Georgie Kemp's URL: https://www.womenintechseo.com/knowledge/optimise-existing-videos-for-ai-search/

When VEED started doing SEO-driven video content years ago, it was purely for traditional search. The unexpected result was that roughly 10% of their AI citations now come from YouTube — not from new content, but from videos published months or years ago that were already well-optimized. The real opportunity isn't creating more content. It's systematically optimizing what you already have.

Recent Ahrefs research analyzing around 75,000 brands found that YouTube mentions outperform every other factor for AI visibility — including domain rating, backlink profile, and brand mentions elsewhere. This holds across ChatGPT, Google's AI Mode, and AI Overviews. And according to BrightEdge, up to 29.5% of Google's AI Overviews cite YouTube, making it the top cited domain overall.

The reason comes down to how AI platforms actually work. It's too expensive for LLMs to parse video directly, so they crawl transcripts and supporting text instead. This means the entire optimization effort should focus on the text layer — transcripts, descriptions, and companion content — not thumbnails or watch time.

Deciding what to optimize first comes down to four questions: Is the video already performing in traditional search? Is anyone still searching for this topic? How much work does it actually need? And does it matter to the business? Videos then sort into three buckets — quick wins with great content but poor metadata, strategic videos worth more investment, and lower-priority content to get to later.

What makes a video citation-worthy is mostly transcript quality. AI platforms have been observed citing videos with half the views of competitors simply because the transcript was cleaner and more readable. That means fixing auto-generated captions, breaking text into proper paragraphs, adding punctuation, using timestamp markers, and adding clarifying notes where the video references something visual like "this chart."

Descriptions should answer real questions upfront — who the video is for, what problem it solves, what they'll learn — with the first 150 characters written as a potential AI snippet answer. Titles should reflect the natural question being answered. Schema markup (VideoObject and HowTo where relevant) reinforces what the video covers and how it fits into a broader content cluster.

Building an ecosystem around each video matters too. Companion blog posts, FAQs, and related resources create multiple citation touchpoints and give AI platforms the text layer they need to understand the content in context. A single optimized video becomes several.

Measuring results splits between traditional signals (organic traffic, rankings, YouTube suggestions, watch time) and AI visibility (manually querying platforms with relevant questions and tracking whether your videos get cited, on which platforms, and how prominently). Leading indicators include what percentage of your library has professional transcripts, proper schema, and question-focused metadata.

Three things to avoid: optimizing for keyword density rather than clarity and usefulness, ignoring the text layer in favor of video-native elements, and optimizing videos in isolation rather than connecting them to blog posts, social content, and email. The ecosystem creates the consensus signal AI platforms look for.

The starting point is simple: audit your top 10 videos honestly, pick three that need minimal work and fix their transcripts and supporting text first, then test whether AI platforms are actually citing them when you ask the questions those videos answer.


